# Partially Ordered Operator Precedence (Coming to you this Zig 0.18.x)
At 6ʰ01 UTC, Zig PR [#30160](https://codeberg.org/ziglang/zig/pulls/30160), was merged into master. 
This PR was my first ever to an open project and it was finally merged in! Yay! But how did it all start, and what is Partially Ordered Operator Precedence, and how did I get here?

## Origin
2024, January 4th, I publicized a small GitHub project entitled [Monopoly-AI](https://github.com/CMDRZero/Monopoly-AI), with the description: 
```
An experiment with using Zig to write a monopoly AI. It would be more 
concisely written in python, but I want to learn zig, and furthermore 
need performance.
```

At that time I was in an Advanced CS class teaching C++. Deciding I was fed up with the strange quirks of C++, I set out to learn another language. 
I found 4 languages I considered worthy: [Nim](https://nim-lang.org/), [Rust](https://rust-lang.org/), [D](https://dlang.org/), and [Zig](https://ziglang.org/).
I then wrote a simple ECC implementation in each language and the first thing that stood out was: "Wow, Zig has any sized integers, that makes sense". In fact this
stood out so much that I added it to my programming language near after, because "If the integers dont have names, but numbers, why should they be restriced to a finite set? What makes a u7 less allowed than a u8?"

From then on I decided to learn Zig, it was eays to pick up (kind of), and felt the most natural. So I began making projects in it. Alot of projects. My old harddrive lists 34 projects made in the years 2024 and 2025.

In general I felt that Zig was forcing me to be a better programmer. I had gotten very used to hacking together what I needed in python, and this made me lazy.
Sometimes lazy is nice, but being correct also solves alot of issues. I began looking at the Zig open issues pages and I often loved seeing genuine discource about how to 
make the language better. And then I began to think to myself: "Well, I love this language alot, what if I was to make a PR".

And then I saw it: [Issue #114](https://github.com/ziglang/zig/issues/114): "require parentheses sometimes to disambiguate confusing operator precedence".

This was an issue I had been facing myself while writing my own toy language. Operators were often unintuitive, and I wasnt sold on 
[Ponylang](https://www.ponylang.io/)'s radical abolition of precedence altogether. Of all of the issues that I felt a connection to, this felt, doable. I hacked together a proof of concept in python, and wow, it was doable.
```
>>> Eval(ToNodeList("1 + 2 | 3"))
Error on line:
0000: 1 + 2 | 3
      --^---^--
Ambigious precedence between operators, please use parentheses
```
In retrospect the code was abysmal and I added features I definately didnt need for a proof of concept, but it worked.

## The Hard Part
Encouraged by my proof of concept, I cloned Zig and tried to build from source. This, it would turn out, is confusing, but only because Im bad at reading.

