Then, on 2025 Sep-18th, I opened [PR #25275](https://github.com/ziglang/zig/pull/25275). 

In retrospect, very few changes have remained the same, but I think theyre interesting so lets talk about them shall we?

## Pratt Parsing
This was the largest hiccup I encountered when I went to implement partial ordering, Zig uses pratt parsing, while my python code had used a wierd strategy. 
What it had done there was to take a flat list of tokens in an expression, then find the highest precedence, and resolve it and put back a node reference.
This meant a linear traversal for every operator as it looked at itself and the previous highest and tried to detect if the relationship was ambiguous. 
This was a terrible strategy, and worse yet, the dreaded comparison tree ended up in Zig.

```python
def Order(a, b):
    r = _Order(a, b)
    if r == None:
        r2 = _Order(b, a)
        if r2 == None: return None
        r = -r2
    return r

def _Order(a, b):
    if a == b: return 0
    if a[0] == 's':
        if b[0] == 's': return 0
        if b[0] in 'pabcl': return +1
    if a[0] == 'p':
        if b[0] == 'p': return 0
        if b[0] in 'abcl': return +1
    if a[0] == 'a':
        if b[0] == 'a':
            if a == 'a0a' and b == 'a1': return +1
            if a == 'a0b' and b == 'a1': return +1
            if a == 'a0a' and b == 'a0b': return 0
        if b[0] in 'cl': return +1
    if a[0] == 'b':
        if b[0] == 'b':
            if a == 'b0' and b == 'b1': return +1
            if a == 'b0' and b == 'b3': return +1
            if a == 'b1' and b == 'b3': return +1
        if b[0] in 'cl': return +1
    if a[0] == 'c':
        if b[0] == 'c': return 0
        if b[0] in 'l': return +1
    if a[0] == 'l':
        if b[0] == 'l':
            if a == 'l0' and b == 'l1': return +1
```

So, what is pratt parsing? Well its a simple way to declaratively specify what precedence different operators have and parse it 
efficiently without duplicating code for recursive descent. 

Lets look at a simplified version of how Zig uses it:
```
fn parseExprPrecedence(p: *Parse, min_prec: i32) Error!?Node.Index {
    assert(min_prec >= 0);
    var node = try p.parsePrefixExpr() orelse return null;
    while (true) {
        const info = //Get Next Operator Info
        if (info.prec < min_prec) break;

        const oper_token = p.nextToken();

        const rhs = try p.parseExprPrecedence(info.prec + 1) orelse { 
            try p.warn(.expected_expr);
            return node;
        };

        node = try p.addNode(.{
            .tag = info.tag,
            .main_token = oper_token,
            .data = .{ .node_and_node = .{ node, rhs } },
        });
    }

    return node;
```

In short, we start with a minimum precedence of the operator before and we injest operators left to right. If it binds looser we dont 
consume it and release the node we have. If it binds the same or higher we then try to recursively call using one higher than that operator. 
As long as every operator binds tighter than a baseline value (0), then it will always eventually succeed. 

Here is a crude diagram
```
      1 + 2 * 3 * 4 ;
min 0 ---
min 1    ----
min 2        ---!
```
When the multiplication recursed, it found itself and rolled back, yielding the `3` node
```
      1 + 2 * 3 * 4 ;
min 0 ---
min 1    ---^   -
min 2            ---!
```
Next our addition call continues, finds the second multiplication and recursed on it, erroring on the semicolon.
```
      1 + 2 * 3 * 4 ;
min 0 ---
min 1   ----^   ^   !
```
Now our addition hits the end and sees it cannot absorb the semicolon. It yields a node to the above.
```
      1 + 2 * 3 * 4 ;
min 0 --^,          -
```
Finally we see the start hits the semicolon and we close out.

So how do we add partial ordering? Well, we can rewrite the above code to have the supplied precedence be cutoff, or `min_prec_exc` as I called it.
This means that we change `info.prec < min_prec` into `info.prec <= min_prec_exc`, and we pass the precedence of the last operator directly. 

Since arithmetic has been removed and we only pass around values themselves, we can shift from integers to abstract order groups. 
In the old version, this was an enum plus an integer pair. This was a mistake and it made the code inscrutible. In the modern version 
we use a single enum which clearly spells out what it corresponds to such as `.arithmetic_product_chainable`.

Additionally, in the modern version, rather than a cryptic tree, we use a comparison matrix which is initialized as comptime with declarative relationship.

This set of legal edges is the heart of partial precedence.

## What is Partial Operator Precedence?
As we've established, most programming languages have an order for all operators, converting any sequence of `A @ B # C $ D` into an ordered series of instruction.
But as we've also established, its not always immediately clear what that order is. What is the result of `A ^ B + C`? Is it even consistant across languages?

So instead partial precedence only declares a relationship between some operators, and if while parsing it needs to figure out which to perform first and there isnt
a relationship, rather than using a cryptic table, it aborts. Those edges look like this graph:

```=html
<pre><code>
                     ┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓   
                     ┃(expr)             ┃                  
                     ┃x() x[] x.y x.* x.?┃                  
                     ┃E!T                ┃                  
                     ┃x{}                ┃                  
                     ┃!x -x -%x ~x &x ?x ┃                  
                     ┗━━━━━━━━━▲━━━━━━━━━┛                  
          ┌─────────────┬──────┴───────┬───────┬──┬──┬─┐         
          │             │              │       │  │  │ │         
   ┏━━━━━━┷━━━━━┓╭Non╌Chaining╮ ╭Non╌Chaining╮┏┷┓┏┷┓┏┷┓│         
   ┃* *% *| / ||┃╎      %     ╎ ╎ &lt;&lt; &lt;&lt;|  >> ╎┃&┃┃^┃┃|┃│         
   ┗━━━━━━▲━━━━━┛╰╌╌╌╌╌╌▲╌╌╌╌╌╯ ╰╌╌╌╌╌╌▲╌╌╌╌╌╯┗▲┛┗▲┛┗▲┛│         
       ┏━━┷━━━━━━━━━━━━━┷━┓       ┌────┴───────┴──┴──┘ │         
       ┃+ - +% -% +| -| ++┃       │                    │         
       ┗━━━━━━━━━━━▲━━━━━━┛       │                    │         
                  ╭┴╌Non-Chaining╌┴╮                   │         
                  ╎== !=  &lt; &lt;= >= >╎                   │         
                  ╰╌╌╌▲╌╌╌╌▲╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╯      ┌──────┬─────┘                
                    ┏━┷━┓┏━┷━┓        ┏━━━┷━━┓┏━━┷━━┓                  
                    ┃and┃┃or ┃        ┃orelse┃┃catch┃                  
                    ┗━▲━┛┗━▲━┛        ┗━━━▲━━┛┗━━▲━━┛                  
                   ╭╌╌┴╌╌╌╌┴╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌┴╌╌╌╌╌╌┴╌╮ 
                   ╎    Any expression location    ╎ 
                   ╰╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╯ 
                                                              
      </code></pre>
```
Every single arrow is an edge, and if there isnt a direct ancestory line from a given operator to another, the precedence is undefined. 
This is what lets us keep some things like classical arithmetic (and extensions) ordered while making the intermixing of bitwise with eachother or
arithmetic an error. 

This is also explained well on the [Zig Langref](https://www.ryanliptak.com/misc/langref-precedence.html). 

## Ok wait, why 400 days?
Long story short, Im not exactly sure, but I knew this was a good addition, so I kept fixing upstream code as it was merged into 
master and collecting a mountain of parenthetizations. Then, in 2025's december or so, Zig moved from Github to Codeberg. At first 
panicked since my PR was frozen, but after I realized what happened, I decided to remove the precedence tree (good riddance), and move to codeberg.

2025 Dec 9th, I opened [Zig PR #30160](https://codeberg.org/ziglang/zig/pulls/30160). At this point in time, my precedence looked different 
than what landed as the team perferred a reduced table. 
The original design for the flowchart above:
```
                        ┌───Suffix─&─Prefix───┐                      
                        │┌───────────────────┐│                      
                        ││x() x[] x.y x.* x.?││                      
                        ││a!b                ││                      
