                               └───┬───┘                             
                                   ▼                                 
   ┌───────────────────────────Assignment───────────────────────────┐
   │┌─────────────────────────Non-Chaining─────────────────────────┐│
   ││= *= *%= *|= /= %= += +%= +|= -= -%= -|= <<= <<|= >>= &= ^= |=││
   │└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘│
   └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```
I stand by my old decision (which is why its this order in LLPC), but I understand the teams decision, its easier to add edges 
than remove them, which breaks backward compatibility; better a big break now than a bunch of small breaks in the future.

So for a long time I just kept keeping my PR up to date. Everyonce in a while I'd see a message informing me to rebase but that was it. I was in the dark.

And then, 2026 Jun 8th, I saw a message that made me beyond excited: 
```
Hey, thanks for working on this, sorry it's taken so long to 
get any attention. I'd like to help get this landed.
    —Ryan Liptak
```

From then on I had a new energy and I began work on a draft of the langref. This was also the time I significantlty overhauled the error messages.
Also since I had been constantly fighting windows I moved my computer to dual boot linux and I have not regretted my decision.

For context, heres what an old message looked like:
```
.../main.zig:16:18: error: ambiguous operator precedence
    return a | b & c;
                 ^
```
VS Now
```
.../main.zig:16:18: error: ambiguous operator precedence with operators … | … & …; use parentheses to disambiguate
    return a | b & c;
                 ^
.../main.zig:16:14: note: previous ambiguous operator here
    return a | b & c;
             ^
.../main.zig:16:18: note: in zig 0.17.x, this was evaluated as `(... | ...) & ...` ?
```

With improved error messages and a langref draft under my belt I moved to making `zig fmt` automatically fix parenthesis.
This was a long battle and ultimately Ryan Liptak wrote most of the final code, but I was able to pull in a fix I had found to a bug that
sometimes duplicated comments.

Then it was a waiting game. I kept the PR up to date, fixed issues when I could, and finally, CI passed and the code was merged.

## Retrospective
This PR was a long time in the making. My first ever PR on any project, and its likely one that people will feel (and post angry messages on Ziggit.dev).

Was it worth it?

Hell yeah, the Zig compiler itself is such a goldmine of code and the high expectations made me improve of a programmer. When I started I had under a year of Zig in my belt.
This december will be my 3rd year of Zig, and Im grateful such a team of talented people is dedicated to making such an amazing language. 

Will I do it again?

Probably, I'm eye a few new PRs to make my next task and hopefully I've shown myself to be competant.

What are you most proud of?

1. Im quite proud of my table driven comparisons. I like the declarative edges and letting everything else fall from that
2. I really like how the error message shows you the ambiguous operators, this involved using a token offset and grabbing the lexemes and I love that it just works :)
3. `zig fmt --upgrade`, I cannot believe I was maintaining this code for 10 months without this to make it a command call.

